---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import MiniMonitor from '../../components/illustrations/MiniMonitor.astro';
import { url } from '../../lib/url';
---

<GuideLayout
  slug="multiple-displays"
  description="How Krova handles more than one monitor: a layout set per monitor, how it recognises a monitor, what happens on reconnect, identical monitors, and why edge snapping ignores the border between screens."
  lead="Every monitor has its own layouts. Krova recognises a monitor when you plug it back in and brings its layouts back."
>
  <figure class="pair" aria-hidden="true">
    <MiniMonitor layout="priority" label="Display 1" />
    <MiniMonitor layout="grid" label="Display 2" />
  </figure>

  <h2 id="per-monitor">One set of layouts per monitor</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The first time Krova sees a monitor, it gives it the four templates (Halves, Thirds, Grid 2x2, Priority) with
      <strong>Halves</strong> active. Edit, add and activate layouts per monitor in
      <a href={url('/guides/layout-editor/')}>the layout editor</a>.
    </li>
    <li>While you drag, only the monitor under your cursor shows its zones, using its own active layout.</li>
    <li>
      Zones are stored as fractions of a monitor, so monitors with different resolutions, shapes or scaling settings can
      share the same idea of a layout. Krova converts per monitor.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="recognised">How a monitor is recognised</h2>
  <p>
    When Krova starts, and whenever your monitors change, it compares each connected monitor with the ones it has saved,
    and scores each pair:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Hardware identity</strong> (the biggest weight). Two monitors with <em>different</em> hardware identities are never treated as the same one.</li>
    <li><strong>Device path</strong>, which identifies the connection the system reports for it, usually the port.</li>
    <li><strong>Last size</strong>, and then <strong>last position</strong>, of the monitor.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Each saved monitor is used once, best matches first. A pair needs a minimum score to count: a matching size alone is
    too weak, but the same size at the same position is enough for a monitor that reports no identity. On Windows the hardware identity is the
    monitor&rsquo;s PnP id (manufacturer and product code, such as <code>DELA0B7</code>), without a serial number. On macOS
    Krova also stores the display&rsquo;s serial number when the display reports one.
  </p>

  <h2 id="reconnect">When you reconnect a monitor</h2>
  <p>
    Unplugging a monitor does not delete its layouts. Krova keeps its saved profile, and when the same monitor returns,
    even in a different position or on a different port, its layouts and active layout come back. A monitor Krova has never
    seen gets a fresh profile with the four templates.
  </p>

  <h2 id="identical">Two identical monitors</h2>
  <p>
    Two monitors of the same model have the same hardware identity on Windows, so Krova tells them apart by device path
    (the port), then by position. In practice the layouts follow the <em>port</em>, not the physical screen. If you swap
    two identical monitors between ports, they can swap layouts. If that happens, open the editor, check each tab, and
    set the active layouts the way you want them. This follows from the matching rules above and has not been tested with
    real identical monitors.
  </p>

  <h2 id="edges">Edge snapping and the border between monitors</h2>
  <p>
    <a href={url('/guides/edge-snapping/')}>Edge snapping</a> never triggers on the shared border between two monitors:
    a side only counts as an edge if no other monitor touches it at the cursor&rsquo;s position. If two monitors are
    staggered, only the part of the border where they touch is ignored; the rest still works.
  </p>

  <h2 id="dpi">Mixed scaling</h2>
  <p>
    On Windows, Krova is designed to work in each monitor&rsquo;s real pixels, so a 100% monitor next to a 150% one is
    handled separately, and the size of a zone is never taken from one monitor and applied to another. Mixed-scale setups
    have had little testing so far.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .pair {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-m);
    max-width: 24rem;
    padding: var(--space-m);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-l);
    background: var(--color-surface);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-1);
  }
</style>
